  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/hyphessobrycon-piranga-2/",
      "html": "<p>This tetra is a beautiful, easy to keep and very lively fish. Males have red fins, while the females resemble black neons (H. herbertaxelrodi) or H. nigricinctus. In the wild the fish are mature at about 2 cm standard length (without fins), but in the aquarium they grow a bit larger; bred fish are about 3 cm long.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/hyphessobrycon-piranga-2/",
      "html": "<p>The species occurs in rivers in the upper rio Tapajós catchment.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/hyphessobrycon-piranga-2/",
      "html": "<p>It was introduced to the hobby by Peter and Martin Hoffmann in the German magazine Datz (issue 11/2017) under the name \"Hyphessobrycon sp. non-red devil\". Despite its many red colours it is obviously not identical with the probably still undescribed species known in the hobby as \"Red Devil\", which develops much larger fins, among other differences. It was officially described in March 2018 as Hyphessobrycon piranga.</p>"
    }
  ],
